WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.880
Joe: Let's now look at how the terms
‘neurodiverse’ and ‘neurotypical’

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:05.830
are used in relation to autism.

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:09.590
The study of the human brain
shows a significant overlap

00:00:09.599 --> 00:00:13.549
between fields of neurology and
psychiatry in the understanding of

00:00:13.549 --> 00:00:15.440
how the brain affects behaviours.

00:00:15.969 --> 00:00:20.220
New terminologies often become part of
common usage to define the differences in

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:24.919
how the world is perceived by different
people and two commonly used terms are

00:00:24.959 --> 00:00:26.610
‘neurodivergent’ and ‘neurotypical’.

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:30.840
Beth: The term ‘neurodiverse’ applies
to individuals who are ‘neurodivergent’.

00:00:32.389 --> 00:00:37.660
The conditions of ADHD, Autism,
Dyspraxia, and Dyslexia make up

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:42.330
neurodiversity, and neuro-differences
are recognised as a social category.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:47.300
As a concept, neurodiversity has been
around for a while and those with

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:52.839
conditions like ADHD and Autism aren't
seen as ‘abnormal’ they're just different.

00:00:53.609 --> 00:00:57.420
The term neurodiverse is used to
describe a variety of conditions

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:01.470
related to cognitive abilities
especially relevant for those Autism.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:06.839
Neurodiverse individuals often struggle
with soft skills, especially ones

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:11.119
that apply to ‘social interactions’
and find physical behaviours like

00:01:11.129 --> 00:01:14.679
standing too close to someone or
speaking too loudly distressing.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:20.550
Neurodiversity is an idea that takes into
account variations in the human brain

00:01:20.559 --> 00:01:26.220
regarding learning, mood, attention,
sociability, and other mental functions

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:30.860
and doesn’t regarded these as abnormal
or unhealthy but as differences

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:32.680
to be understood and worked with.

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:37.370
Neurodiversity reflects the idea
that Autism is not a mental health

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:42.000
problem and rejects the medical model
of disability and whilst not all

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:46.289
individuals with Autism apply this term
to themselves it can help more people

00:01:46.299 --> 00:01:48.329
on the spectrum to live fuller lives.

00:01:49.119 --> 00:01:52.320
Joe: The term ‘neurotypical’ started
to be used around the same time as

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:56.690
neurodiversity as a means of describing
those who don’t display autistic or

00:01:56.750 --> 00:02:00.729
other ‘atypical ‘patterns of thoughts
or behaviour, a way of describing

00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:02.929
people without any particular condition.

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:07.610
Neurotypical describes individuals
who display ‘typical’ intellectual

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:11.080
and cognitive development and are
on the whole social and thrive

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:12.109
from interacting with each other.

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:17.500
They acquire physical, verbal,
intellectual, and social skills usually

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:21.370
at a specific pace and meet standardly
accepted milestones for development.

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:26.810
Neurotypical people also display commonly
accepted physical behaviours such as

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:31.100
being able to speak whilst modulating
their volume and don’t find such things as

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:35.160
maintaining eye contact distressing, and
can function without becoming overloaded

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:37.020
by stimuli in distracting situations.

00:02:37.759 --> 00:02:41.589
Beth: However the term ‘typical’ can
vary from person to person and within

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:46.090
cultures, also some individuals can
experience an overlap in what is

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:48.739
considered neurodiverse and neurotypical.

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:53.769
Joe: But the meaning of both terms is
constantly evolving, the term neurotypical

00:02:54.009 --> 00:02:58.200
is an improvement over the term
‘normal’ but how do we define typical.

00:02:58.869 --> 00:03:02.690
An individual is unique by nature,
and creating a dividing line between

00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:06.550
those who are Neuro-diverse and those
who are Neurotypical is not easy,

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:08.440
but should it even be necessary?

